WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.385
[SQUEAKING][RUSTLING][CLICKING]

00:00:06.180 --> 00:00:08.430
CLAIRE HALLORAN: Today we're
going to do Goodie Bag 4,

00:00:08.430 --> 00:00:09.450
VSEPR.

00:00:09.450 --> 00:00:12.210
Our objective is to visualize
the three dimensional

00:00:12.210 --> 00:00:15.330
structure of some simple
chemical compounds.

00:00:15.330 --> 00:00:18.320
The only thing you'll need
is a molecular modeling kit.

00:00:18.320 --> 00:00:20.610
A conceptual question you
should think about today

00:00:20.610 --> 00:00:25.130
is, what factors determine how
bonds rotate in a molecule?

00:00:25.130 --> 00:00:26.630
So the first thing
we're going to do

00:00:26.630 --> 00:00:29.500
is draw the Lewis structure
for this molecule.

00:00:29.500 --> 00:00:34.260
So we know that Si, S, and CN,
since they have between three

00:00:34.260 --> 00:00:36.390
and five valence
electrons, make up

00:00:36.390 --> 00:00:38.280
the backbone of this molecule.

00:00:38.280 --> 00:00:40.320
And then we're going
to add all of the atoms

00:00:40.320 --> 00:00:42.300
around each of these
backbone atoms.

00:00:46.580 --> 00:00:48.350
Then counting valence
electrons, we're

00:00:48.350 --> 00:00:50.750
going to make sure that
we have the correct number

00:00:50.750 --> 00:00:55.390
of non-bonding pairs in bonds.

00:00:55.390 --> 00:00:57.940
And this will allow us
to identify the geometry

00:00:57.940 --> 00:01:00.130
at each atom in the backbone.

00:01:00.130 --> 00:01:07.570
So this silicon bond here
has four bonding domains,

00:01:07.570 --> 00:01:09.670
and all of them are
bonded to another atom.

00:01:09.670 --> 00:01:14.610
So we know that this is going to
be a test tetragonal geometry.

00:01:14.610 --> 00:01:18.030
This bond here, centered
around the sulfur,

00:01:18.030 --> 00:01:23.040
has five body domains, and one
of them is a non-bonding pair.

00:01:23.040 --> 00:01:26.250
We know that this will
be a seesaw geometry.

00:01:26.250 --> 00:01:29.790
And then, finally, this
carbon has two bonding domains

00:01:29.790 --> 00:01:31.900
with atoms in each
of those domains,

00:01:31.900 --> 00:01:33.925
so this will be a
linear geometry.

00:01:33.925 --> 00:01:35.550
And now we're ready
to build our model.

00:01:57.250 --> 00:01:59.370
So first, we're going to
draw the Lewis structure

00:01:59.370 --> 00:02:00.720
for this molecule.

00:02:00.720 --> 00:02:03.420
As with before, we're going
to identify the backbone

00:02:03.420 --> 00:02:05.770
atoms in this molecule.

00:02:05.770 --> 00:02:11.700
So in this case, the backward
atoms are C, B, and O.

00:02:11.700 --> 00:02:14.100
And now we're going to attach
the other atoms to them.

00:02:20.210 --> 00:02:21.960
And now we're going
to check and make sure

00:02:21.960 --> 00:02:24.060
that there are the proper
number of valence electrons

00:02:24.060 --> 00:02:24.768
around each atom.

00:02:28.530 --> 00:02:30.720
Next, we're going to
identify the geometry

00:02:30.720 --> 00:02:32.770
at each of the backbone atoms.

00:02:32.770 --> 00:02:36.000
So the first geometry centered
around this carbon atom here

00:02:36.000 --> 00:02:38.550
has four bonding
domains, all of which

00:02:38.550 --> 00:02:40.630
contain bonds to other atoms.

00:02:40.630 --> 00:02:42.430
So that's going
to be tetragonal.

00:02:42.430 --> 00:02:46.170
The second geometry has three
boding domains, all of which

00:02:46.170 --> 00:02:47.590
are bonded to other atoms.

00:02:47.590 --> 00:02:51.600
So this is going to be a
trigonal planar geometry.

00:02:51.600 --> 00:02:55.710
And, finally, the geometry
around this oxygen atom

00:02:55.710 --> 00:02:59.910
has four bonding domains, two
of which are bonded to atoms,

00:02:59.910 --> 00:03:02.440
and two of which are
occupied by lone pairs.

00:03:02.440 --> 00:03:05.790
So that's going to
be a bent geometry.

00:03:05.790 --> 00:03:07.714
And now we're ready to
construct our model.

00:03:26.510 --> 00:03:29.180
The Goodie Bag 4
worksheet asks us,

00:03:29.180 --> 00:03:32.040
whether some different bonds
can be in the same place?

00:03:32.040 --> 00:03:39.290
So first, it asks, whether
this H, Si, Si, S, and CN bond

00:03:39.290 --> 00:03:40.890
can be in the same plane?

00:03:40.890 --> 00:03:43.340
Remember that the CN
bond cannot rotate,

00:03:43.340 --> 00:03:44.870
because it's a triple bond.

00:03:44.870 --> 00:03:50.780
But we can rotate around this
S, Si bond, and get all three

00:03:50.780 --> 00:03:52.850
of the bonds to be
in the same plane.

00:03:52.850 --> 00:03:54.960
The plane shown
with my hand here.

00:03:54.960 --> 00:03:56.910
So here is our first bond.

00:03:56.910 --> 00:03:58.320
Here's our second bond.

00:03:58.320 --> 00:03:59.760
And here's our third bond.

00:03:59.760 --> 00:04:01.250
All on the same plane.

00:04:01.250 --> 00:04:07.010
Next, it asks if we can
have this H, Si, S, F,

00:04:07.010 --> 00:04:09.500
and CN bond in the same plane?

00:04:09.500 --> 00:04:12.825
And so we can try to
rotate to achieve this.

00:04:12.825 --> 00:04:14.450
But we notice that
this isn't possible,

00:04:14.450 --> 00:04:17.950
because these two bonds are
always in the same plane,

00:04:17.950 --> 00:04:20.733
and we can't get any of
these bonds into that plane,

00:04:20.733 --> 00:04:22.025
because of the seesaw geometry.

00:04:25.620 --> 00:04:28.650
So for our second molecule
here, the Goodie Bag worksheet

00:04:28.650 --> 00:04:30.390
asks a different question.

00:04:30.390 --> 00:04:33.780
We notice that this
central CB bond can rotate,

00:04:33.780 --> 00:04:36.750
and the Goodie Bag asks,
which configuration

00:04:36.750 --> 00:04:38.380
is the lowest energy?

00:04:38.380 --> 00:04:40.470
So remember that the
entire VSEPR model

00:04:40.470 --> 00:04:43.890
is centered around the idea that
we want to minimize repulsion

00:04:43.890 --> 00:04:46.350
between electron
clouds by maximizing

00:04:46.350 --> 00:04:47.860
the distance between them.

00:04:47.860 --> 00:04:50.730
So we're going to rotate
this so that the repulsion

00:04:50.730 --> 00:04:54.240
between these groups in the
molecules are minimized.

00:04:54.240 --> 00:04:56.310
So as we rotate this,
we notice that there are

00:04:56.310 --> 00:04:57.990
two different possibilities.

00:04:57.990 --> 00:05:01.680
Remember that these
green atoms are CL,

00:05:01.680 --> 00:05:05.610
and this yellow atom is
oxygen. These are the largest

00:05:05.610 --> 00:05:09.000
atoms in our model, and also
the ones that have the most

00:05:09.000 --> 00:05:10.890
repulsive electron clouds.

00:05:10.890 --> 00:05:12.360
So we're going to
want to maximize

00:05:12.360 --> 00:05:14.050
the distance between them.

00:05:14.050 --> 00:05:15.720
So one way that
this can be achieved

00:05:15.720 --> 00:05:18.750
is by rotating the model so
that the oxygen is aligned

00:05:18.750 --> 00:05:22.260
with the hydrogen, which has
a very small electron cloud,

00:05:22.260 --> 00:05:25.980
and thus is capable of only
very slight propulsion.

00:05:25.980 --> 00:05:28.740
So this configuration
is low energy,

00:05:28.740 --> 00:05:32.220
because the very repulsive
chlorine atoms are far

00:05:32.220 --> 00:05:34.350
from the repulsive oxygen atom.

00:05:34.350 --> 00:05:37.320
However, there's also a
different possibility.

00:05:37.320 --> 00:05:41.100
If we rotate, we can put
the oxygen atom in a plane

00:05:41.100 --> 00:05:43.480
in between the two
chlorine atoms,

00:05:43.480 --> 00:05:47.190
so that the oxygen is not
directly facing an atom.

00:05:47.190 --> 00:05:51.240
However, this configuration
puts the oxygen atom

00:05:51.240 --> 00:05:54.870
spatially closer to a
chlorine atom, as shown here,

00:05:54.870 --> 00:06:00.300
and this hydrogen atom is very
close to this chlorine atom.

00:06:00.300 --> 00:06:05.100
So the first configuration
is lower energy.

00:06:05.100 --> 00:06:07.110
Today, we used our
molecular modeling kids

00:06:07.110 --> 00:06:10.710
to visualize the 3D structure
of some simple molecules using

00:06:10.710 --> 00:06:12.690
the VSEPR model.

00:06:12.690 --> 00:06:15.090
By playing with these
bonds and rotating them,

00:06:15.090 --> 00:06:18.510
we were able to understand
how repulsion between atoms

00:06:18.510 --> 00:06:22.080
gives rise to the lowest
energy configurations

00:06:22.080 --> 00:06:24.230
of these molecules.

